PythonC++Command line <pre><code>sphere = wm.read_mesh(str(DATA / "sphere.obj"))

# Memory: inputs by name, nothing written; returns the output meshes and a report.
outputs, report = wm.qslim({"input": "sphere", "target_rel": 0.5}, {"sphere": sphere})
print(list(outputs), report["vertices"], "vertices")

# Files: as wmtk_app does with the same options; returns nothing.
wm.qslim({"input": str(DATA / "sphere.obj"), "output": "sphere_half.obj", "target_rel": 0.5})
</code></pre> <p><code>wm.&lt;component&gt;(options)</code> works on files and returns nothing; <code>wm.&lt;component&gt;(options, meshes)</code> works in memory and returns <code>(outputs, report)</code>. <code>outputs["mesh"]</code> is the result; some components return more meshes (TetWild also returns <code>surface</code>, its tracked surface, and <code>envelope</code>). The component releases the Python GIL while it runs; calls run one at a time per process.</p> <pre><code>const wmtk::MeshMap meshes{{"sphere", wmtk::io::read_mesh(data / "sphere.obj")}};
auto [outputs, report] =
    wmtk::components::qslim::qslim({{"input", "sphere"}, {"target_rel", 0.5}}, meshes);
std::cout &lt;&lt; report["vertices"] &lt;&lt; " vertices\n";
wmtk::io::write_mesh("sphere_half.msh", outputs.at("mesh"));
</code></pre> <p>Each component has both overloads:</p> <pre><code>void name(nlohmann::json options);
ComponentResult name(nlohmann::json options, const MeshMap&amp; meshes);
</code></pre> <p><code>MeshMap</code> is <code>std::map&lt;std::string, MeshData&gt;</code>; <code>ComponentResult</code> is the pair of output meshes and the JSON report.</p> <p><code>wmtk_app -j options.json</code> runs the file route. <code>application</code> names the component; input paths are relative to the JSON file, and outputs are written to the working directory.</p> <p>In memory, the options name inputs instead of files (<code>"input": "sphere"</code>) and need no <code>application</code>, <code>output</code> or <code>input_dir</code>.</p>
